#!/bin/bash
# ntp/help.loc - Module-specific help

cat <<'EOF'
Module: ntp - time synchronization via chrony

  For ON-LAN hosts. Points them at a local time server while LEAVING the
  distribution's public pool in place as a fallback, so losing the local
  server does not leave every host with no time source at all.

  Off-LAN or minimal hosts do not need this module. Ubuntu runs
  systemd-timesyncd by default, which is accurate enough for logging and for
  apt's Release-file validity checks. An offsite backup box should skip it.

SUBCOMMANDS

  setup                      Install chrony, write drop-in config, verify sync
      yamc -h HOST -u root ntp

  status                     Report clock, daemon and sources. Read-only, and
                             works on timesyncd hosts too.
      yamc -h HOST -u root ntp status

  cleanup                    Undo the in-place chrony.conf edits made by older
                             versions of this module. Only needed on hosts
                             configured before the rewrite; 'setup' refuses to
                             run until it has been done.
      yamc -h HOST -u root ntp cleanup

CONFIGURATION  (yamc.local/ntp/config.conf)

  NTP_SERVERS="ntp1 ntp2"    preferred local servers (space-separated)
  NTP_POOL=pool.ntp.org      override the fallback pool (normally unset)
  NTP_IS_SERVER=true         also serve time to the network
  NTP_ALLOW="192.168.0.0/16" REQUIRED when serving - no default
  NTP_LOCAL_STRATUM=10       stratum to advertise while unsynced

  NTP_SERVER (singular) is still accepted.

HOW CONFIG IS APPLIED

  Drop-in files, not edits to chrony.conf:

    /etc/chrony/sources.d/yamc.sources     server / pool lines
    /etc/chrony/conf.d/yamc-server.conf    allow / local stratum

  So it is idempotent, reversible by deleting the file, and survives chrony
  package upgrades. Source changes reload without restarting the daemon;
  conf.d changes need a restart, and the module does the lesser of the two.

  Local servers get 'prefer', so hosts agree with each other while the
  packaged pool covers a local outage.

NOTES

  - Installing chrony DISPLACES systemd-timesyncd. That is a daemon swap, not
    just a config change; the module reports the resulting state.
  - Original config is preserved as /etc/chrony/chrony.conf.orig
EOF
